use /lib
from /ui/theme import Theme

export feature DragReorderBehaviour:
    pitch:
        How anything that can be reordered by dragging shows where it will
        end up, as macOS lists and tab bars do: with a line in the gap between
        two items, never a highlighted item that leaves the drop ambiguous.
        Every list, tab bar, or row of items that is reordered by drag and
        drop behaves this way.

    description:
        While an item is dragged over the items it can be reordered among,
        what it would be dropped into is always a gap between two items, or
        before the first or after the last, never an item itself. That gap is
        shown by an insertion indicator, and nothing else marks the drop: no
        item is highlighted, tinted, or outlined as a target.

    indicator:
        The indicator is a line 2 pixels thick in the @{Theme}'s accent,
        with rounded ends and a small open circle, 6 pixels across and in the
        same colour, at its leading end. It lies across the gap, centred on
        the gap, horizontal between the rows of a list, running the list's
        width less its padding, and vertical between the tabs of a tab bar or
        the items of a row, running their height. It is drawn over the items,
        taking no room of its own, so nothing moves to make space while the
        drag is under way, and it never takes the mouse.

        A user may open the gap instead, as its user says: the items either
        side slide apart to make room where the item would land, and what
        that user says of the gap, the dragged copy, and the drop holds there
        in place of what is said here.

    choosingTheGap:
        Which gap is shown follows the pointer: over the first half of an
        item, the gap before it; over the second half, the gap after it,
        halves taken along the direction the items run. Past the last item,
        the gap after it; before the first, the gap before it. The two gaps
        either side of the dragged item itself are where it already is, so
        neither shows an indicator, and dropping there changes nothing. A gap
        an item may not be dropped into, as its user says, shows no indicator
        either, and nothing is marked while the pointer is over it.

        The indicator moves from gap to gap at once as the pointer crosses the
        middle of an item, with no animation, so it is always exactly where a
        drop would land.

    betweenLists:
        Where its user lets items move between lists, as the columns of a
        board, an item is dragged from one list into a gap of another as it
        is within its own. The gap is chosen, as above, among the items of
        the list the pointer is over, and the indicator is drawn across that
        list, running its width less its padding, never across the list the
        item came from. A list with no items has one gap, at its top, so an
        empty list takes a drop there, the indicator drawn across its top
        beneath its heading. A list its user says takes no drops shows no
        indicator anywhere over it, and an item dropped on it goes nowhere;
        its own items can't be picked up to be dragged.

    dragging:
        The item dragged follows the pointer as a small floating copy, the
        item's own label on a raised surface with a soft shadow, as its user
        says. The item stays where it was in the list, dimmed to half its
        opacity, until it is dropped.

    scrollingWhileDragging:
        Items that scroll can be reordered past what is in view, so an item
        can be dragged to any gap, however long the list. While an item is
        dragged, held near an edge of the scrolling area the items run along,
        within 32 pixels of its top or bottom for a list, or of its left or
        right for a row, the area scrolls that way on its own, slowly at
        first and faster the nearer the pointer is to the edge, or the
        further past it, up to about a row every 50 milliseconds, smoothly
        frame by frame rather than in jumps, and stops at its end. Moving
        the pointer away from the edge stops it at once. The wheel and
        trackpad scroll the area too while the item is dragged, as they do
        at any time, without dropping it.

        However the area scrolls, the dragged copy stays under the pointer,
        and the gap shown is chosen anew each frame from where the pointer is
        over the items now there, so the indicator is always exactly where a
        drop would land. An area that doesn't scroll, or holds every item in
        view, doesn't scroll on its own.

        Where lists sit side by side in an area that scrolls the other way,
        as the columns of a board, each scrolls as above: held within 32
        pixels of the outer area's left or right edge, the area scrolls
        sideways, bringing the lists beyond into view, and held within 32
        pixels of a list's own top or bottom, that list scrolls, and only
        it. Held near a corner, both scroll at once, each at its own pace.

    dropping:
        Released over a gap that shows the indicator, the item moves into that
        gap, the items between shifting over by one. Released anywhere else,
        outside the items, over a gap it may not go into, or back where it
        started, nothing changes. Either way the indicator and the dimming go
        at once.

